<Group labelreference="L3" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field style="Title" layout="Title"><Font underline="false">Using Maple to solve ODEs</Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Author" layout="Author">Dr S Crampin</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">The aim of this worksheet is to introduce the use of Maple for the numerical solution of ordinary differential equations. The way to use this worksheet is as follows. You should read through and execute  the commands where indicated. Use the [&gt;_] button on the menu bar to create additional Maple prompts where you can enter  commands, for example to undertake the suggested exercises. As well as the examples that are given on the worksheet, do enter your own variations, to get more practice. If you have any questions or problems, ask for help from the lecturer or postgraduate demonstrator.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">There is no coursework directly associated with this worksheet. However, both the next topic, the shooting method, and your mini-project, will exploit the techniques covered here.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Keep this worksheet safe - you may need it for future reference.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">1. Initiating Maple</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">See previous sheets. For the activities on this sheet, my preference is to use Worksheet document mode.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 1" layout="Heading 1">2. Numerical solution of ordinary differential equations</Text-field></Title>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.1 A physical problem</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Although Maple is proficient at finding analytic solutions to differential equations, there are many equations to which it cannot find the exact solution. Consider as an example the equation of motion for a classical particle of mass <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> attached to a spring and able to move along the <Font style="Text" italic="true">x</Font>-axis, with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> the equilibrium position. A onventional spring has potential energy <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, where k is the spring constant. Let the spring here have (in general) unusual properties, with <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> not proportional to <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>but proportional to <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> where <Equation executable="true" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYnLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJTBmb250X3N0eWxlX25hbWVHUSsyRH5Db21tZW50RicvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYnLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnLyUrZXhlY3V0YWJsZUdRJmZhbHNlRicvJTBmb250X3N0eWxlX25hbWVHUSsyRH5Db21tZW50RicvRjNRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> is a positive integer. We take the proportionality constant to be unity for convenience, so that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> . To help visualise the problem, we plot the potential for various values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation>:</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L4" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plot([x^2,x^4,x^6,x^10,x^20],x=-1.5..1.5,0..2, legend=[&quot;2&quot;,&quot;4&quot;,&quot;6&quot;,&quot;10&quot;,&quot;20&quot;]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="500.0" originalheight="500.0" type="two-dimensional" width="500.0" originalwidth="500.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="true" input="_ATTRIBUTE(&quot;input&quot; = [TABLE([1 = plot, 2 = [x^2, x^4, x^6, x^10, x^20], 3 = (x = -1.5 .. 1.5), 4 = 0 .. 2, 5 = (legend = [&quot;2&quot;, &quot;4&quot;, &quot;6&quot;, &quot;10&quot;, &quot;20&quot;])]), &quot;originalview&quot; = [-1.50000000000000000 .. 1.50000000000000000, 0.529939713449227995e-43 .. 3325.25673007965088], &quot;originalaxesticks&quot; = AXESTICKS(DEFAULT, DEFAULT, _ATTRIBUTE(&quot;source&quot; = &quot;mathdefault&quot;))])"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L44" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">For <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> we have the usual harmonic oscillator potential describing a spring behaving according to Hooke's Law. As <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy8lJ2ZhbWlseUdRMFRpbWVzfk5ld35Sb21hbkYnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation> increases, the system more and more resembles a particle free to move in a box with confining walls at  <Font style="Text" italic="true">x=</Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>,+1. You have studied the behaviour of a quantum particle in such a system in your quantum physics courses. Here we consider a classical particle. You might like to contrast the behaviour exhibited in each case.</Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.1.1 Exercise</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Do you understand what is going on? Then how would you change the potential so that as <Font style="Text" italic="true">p</Font> became larger and larger the system more and more resembles a particle moving in a box of width 4, rather than 2? </Text-field>
</Section>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.2 Can Maple find analytic solutions?</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">The force experienced by the particle is given by  <Font style="Text" italic="true">F(x)=</Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> =<Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, so that Newton's Second law of Motion  <Font style="Text" italic="true">F=ma </Font>leads to the differential equation  <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Let us try to find the solution corresponding to a particle passing through <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> at <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, with velocity <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. Using the <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve</Font> command (section 4.2 on worksheet 2) Maple succeeds in finding an analytic solution when <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. First the general solution for this case:</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L5" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">p:=1;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSJwR0YoIiIiNyNGLg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L6" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eqn:=diff(x(t),t,t)=-2*p*x(t)^(2*p-1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSRlcW5HRigvLUklZGlmZkdGJjYkLUkieEdGKDYjSSJ0R0YoLUkiJEdGJjYkRjUiIiMsJEYyISIjNyMvLUYwNiQtRjA2JEYyRjVGNUY6</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L7" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">dsolve(eqn,x(t));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Ly1JInhHNiI2I0kidEdGJSwmKiZJJWNfXzFHRiUiIiItSSRzaW5HNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHRiU2IyomIiIjI0YrRjNGJ0YrRitGKyomSSVjX18yR0YlRistSSRjb3NHRi5GMUYrRis=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L45" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Note that for clarity I have defined <Font style="Text" italic="true">eqn</Font> to be the equation to be solved, rather than type the equation in the <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve</Font> command itself. I recommend you follow this practice as it leads to clearer workings.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Do you understand the solution that Maple has given for the equation?</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Now we find the solution to the equation which satisfies the initial conditions. Again, for convenience the initial conditions are defined as a separate variable (<Font style="Text" italic="true">ICs</Font>) before calling <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve</Font>, rather than entering them into the command directly.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L8" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ICs:=x(0)=0,D(x)(0)=1;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSRJQ3NHRig2JC8tSSJ4R0YoNiMiIiFGMy8tLUkiREdGJTYjRjFGMiIiIjckRi9GNA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L9" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">dsolve({eqn,ICs},x(t));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Ly1JInhHNiI2I0kidEdGJSwkKiYiIiMjIiIiRiotSSRzaW5HNiQlKnByb3RlY3RlZEdJKF9zeXNsaWJHRiU2IyomRipGK0YnRixGLEYr</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L46" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Notice how the boundary condition for the velocity is specified - recall the <Font style="Text" italic="true">D</Font> command (section 5, worksheet 1) returns the derivative of the argument, here the function <Font style="Text" italic="true">x(t)</Font>. </Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">If we repeat but this time for a larger value of <Font style="Text" italic="true">p,</Font> we find an integral returned for the general solution (if typing in the lines on this wrksheet, cut and paste from the lines you have previously entered above to save typing in lengthy lines again):</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L10" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">p:=3;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSJwR0YoIiIkNyNGLg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L11" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eqn:=diff(x(t),t,t)=-2*p*x(t)^(2*p-1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM+SSRlcW5HRigvLUklZGlmZkdGJjYkLUkieEdGKDYjSSJ0R0YoLUkiJEdGJjYkRjUiIiMsJCokRjIiIiYhIic3Iy8tRjA2JC1GMDYkRjJGNUY1Rjo=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L12" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">dsolve(eqn,x(t));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L47" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">This isn't particularly useful.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Furthermore Maple fails to find a useable solution when we ask for a particular solution satisfying our specific boundary conditions:</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L13" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">dsolve({eqn,ICs},x(t));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L48" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Clearly, for this problem and others we will need to adopt a numerical approach.</Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.3 Numerical solution using the Euler method</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">To illustrate the nature of numerical solutions to differential equations, let us investigate the solution of this problem using the Euler method (which Maple refers to as the <Font style="Text" italic="true">forward Euler</Font> method). This is not a method recommended for actual ``real world&quot; problems, but it serves to illustrate some of the numerical issues very clearly. </Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">The Maple command to perform a numerical solution is also <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve, </Font>but with the <Font style="Text" italic="true">numeric</Font> option specified.  The result of executing the command is not the numerical solution, but a procedure which can then be used to generate it. For example, to solve the mass on a spring problem using the forward Euler method we enter the following. (We consider the case  <Font style="Text" italic="true">p=1</Font> - we know the exact solution for this case so we can check our solution is correct. In undertaking numerical work it is good practice wherever possible to check that your numerical methods are correct by comparing with known cases/solutions.)</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L14" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">p:=1:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L15" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eqn:=diff(x(t),t,t)=-2*p*x(t)^(2*p-1):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L16" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ICs:=x(0)=0,D(x)(0)=1:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L17" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">soln:=dsolve({eqn,ICs},x(t),numeric,method=classical[foreuler], stepsize=0.01);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L49" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">The name <Font style="Text" italic="true">soln</Font> is the name that I have chosen to hold the result of the procedure - it could have been any unreserved name. The option <Font style="Text" italic="true">stepsize=0.01</Font> is used to specify the value of the step size <Font style="Text" italic="true">h</Font> to be used in the Euler method. Evaluating the procedure at some argument <Font style="Text" italic="true">t</Font> we get a list of equations, each corresponding to the value of the independent variable or a dependent function, set equal to their  numerical value. </Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L18" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">soln(5.0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM3JS9JInRHRigkIiNdISIiLy1JInhHRig2I0YuJCIwMl5DXSoqb0UmISM6Ly1JJWRpZmZHRiY2JEYzRi4kIjAoKmVqRSRmPXVGODcjNyVGLS9GM10zRkUwREFBNEUxRURFODNGL0Y6XTNGRTdCRDRGQzU0NDQ3QzU=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L50" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Notice how we get told <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and not just <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>. This is a clue to the fact that Maple took our original second order ordinary differential equation and converted it into the standard dynamical form (see introductory presentation) before solving.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">To  plot the solution we can make use of a special <Font style="Text" italic="true">odeplot</Font> command that forms part of the <Font style="Text" italic="true">plots</Font> package. It takes the output from a <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve </Font>command and plots it:</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L19" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">with(plots):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L20" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">odeplot(soln,[t,x(t)],0..10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="500.0" originalheight="500.0" type="two-dimensional" width="500.0" originalwidth="500.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false" input=""></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L74" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">The solution looks like we might expect. It oscillates between +/-0.7, which compares well with the  <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>  value of the prefactor in the exact solution found earlier. The frequency of oscillation is also consistent.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">We can solve the same physical problem as two coupled first order differential equations, by transforming it into the standard dynamical form. That is what the next few lines of code do. Make sure you understand how to transform this second-order ODE into these two first-order ODEs:</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L21" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eqns:=diff(x(t),t)=v(t),diff(v(t),t)=-2*p*x(t)^(2*p-1):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L22" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ICs:=x(0)=0,v(0)=1:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L23" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">soln:=dsolve({eqns,ICs},{x(t),v(t)},numeric, method=classical[foreuler],stepsize=0.01):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L24" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">soln(5.0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyM3JS9JInRHRigkIiNdISIiLy1JInZHRig2I0YuJCIwKCplakUkZj11ISM6Ly1JInhHRihGNSQiMDJeQ10qKm9FJkY4NyM3JUYtL0YzXTNGRTdCRDRGQzU0NDQ3QzUvRjpdM0ZFMERBQTRFMUVERTgzRg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L75" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Comparing with the previous example of evaluating the procedure at some particular <Font style="Text" italic="true">t</Font>, the only difference is that Maple now  calls  <Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>  the quantity  <Font style="Text" italic="true">v(t)</Font>.  Clearly it did solve the differential equation before via the dynamical form even though we did not ask it to (clever!).</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Since we now have both <Font style="Text" italic="true">x(t)</Font> and <Font style="Text" italic="true">v(t)</Font> we can plot out the energy, which with a mass of <Font style="Text" italic="true">m=</Font>1 is given by <Font style="Text" italic="true">E=</Font>k.e.+p.e.<Font style="Text" italic="true">=</Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>/2+V or <Font style="Text" italic="true">E=</Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>/2+<Equation executable="false" style="2D Comment" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L25" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">odeplot(soln,[t,v(t)^2/2+(x(t))^(2*p)],0..10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="500.0" originalheight="500.0" type="two-dimensional" width="500.0" originalwidth="500.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false" input=""></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L76" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Oh dear! Even though the oscillations looked fine, in actual fact the mass is gradually gaining more and more energy, which must be due to the inaccuracies of the Euler method because the total energy of our system should be constant. When simulating dynamical systems, ensuring your numerical scheme is consistent with requirements such as conservation of energy is very important.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">In fact, if we had looked carefully (maybe you did!) at the plot we first generated of the solution of the equation, you may have noticed that the amplitude of the oscillation was not constant. To highlight this, let us overlay the numerical and exact result (found previously) - note the colons at the end of the first two lines, to suppress output.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L26" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plot_exact:=plot(sin(sqrt(2.0)*t)/sqrt(2.0),t=0..10,color=blue):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L27" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plot_numerical:=odeplot(soln,[t,x(t)],0..10,color=red):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L28" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">display(plot_exact,plot_numerical);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="500.0" originalheight="500.0" type="two-dimensional" width="500.0" originalwidth="500.0" plot-scale="1.0" plot-xtrans="0.0" plot-ytrans="0.0" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="false" input=""></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L77" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">There is one other particularly useful  option to the <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve</Font> command, which enables one to access individual dependent variables. This is the <Font style="Text" italic="true">output=listprocedure</Font> option.  When specified, the resulting procedure can then be used in a <Font style="Text" italic="true">subs</Font> command to  produce functions/procedures of  individual variables, rather than the list we have been getting so far e.g. when executing <Font style="Text" italic="true">soln(5.0)</Font> above.  Here we create a function <Font style="Text" italic="true">velocity</Font> which returns the velocity of our mass-on-a-spring, and use it to find the velocity at t=2 and t=5.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Group labelreference="L29" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">p:=5:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L30" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eqns:=diff(x(t),t)=v(t),diff(v(t),t)=-2*p*x(t)^(2*p-1):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L31" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ICs:=x(0)=0,v(0)=1:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L32" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">soln:=dsolve({eqns,ICs},{x(t),v(t)},numeric, method=classical[foreuler],stepsize=0.01,output=listprocedure):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L33" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">velocity:=subs(soln,v(t));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group hide-input="false" labelreference="L34" drawlabel="true">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">velocity(2.0);velocity(5.0);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="LUkjbW5HNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JFE6JnVtaW51czA7MS4wNzg2ODgzODM1ODk2NUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyMkITBsKmUkUSlveTUhIzk3I11CRkYxNDI0RUMwMjE3Qjg5</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output><Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="LUkjbW5HNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JFE7JnVtaW51czA7MC42NTI3ODI2Mzc0MzY0NjhGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LV9JLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nRzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiSSxtcHJpbnRzbGFzaEdGKDYkNyMkITBva1ZQRXlfJyEjOjcjXUJGRTRFMzk4NjlFNUY1Q0E=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L73" drawlabel="true" applyint="true" applyrational="true" applyexponent="false">
<Input><Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal"><Equation executable="true" style="2D Input" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2I1EhRicvJSdmYW1pbHlHUTBUaW1lc35OZXd+Um9tYW5GJy8lK2V4ZWN1dGFibGVHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">JSFH</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.3.1 Exercise</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Set <Font style="Text" italic="true">p</Font> equal to 1. Use Maple to create a function <Font style="Text" italic="true">energy</Font> (along the lines of <Font style="Text" italic="true">velocity</Font> above) which can be used to find the energy at a time that it takes as an argument. Use it to calculate the <Font underline="true" style="Text">error</Font>  in the energy at <Font style="Text" italic="true">t=10</Font>, by comparing the calculated and initial (exact) energy. Repeat the calculation but this time decreasing the step size by a factor of two. What happens to the error in the energy? Decrease the step size again  to confirm the relationship between the step size and the error.</Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.3.2 Exercise</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Repeat the last calculations for <Font style="Text" italic="true">p=5 </Font>and <Font style="Text" italic="true">p=10.</Font> Use the <Font style="Text" italic="true">odeplot </Font>command to plot <Font style="Text" italic="true">x(t)</Font> against <Font style="Text" italic="true">t</Font>, <Font style="Text" italic="true">v(t)</Font> against <Font style="Text" italic="true">t</Font>, and the energy against <Font style="Text" italic="true">t</Font> - you can vary the number of points Maple uses in the plot by including the option <Font style="Text" italic="true">numpoints=N</Font> in the <Font style="Text" italic="true">odeplot</Font> command, where <Font style="Text" italic="true">N</Font> is the number required - e.g. 200 or 400 might give better results in this case. Think about the  solutions that you have plotted, both the physical behaviour and also in terms of the approximations made in the Euler method.</Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.4 Improved accuracy with the Runge-Kutta method</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Maple can solve differential equations using the second-order or fourth-order Runge-Kutta method by specifying the option <Font style="Text" italic="true">method=classical[rk2]</Font> or <Font style="Text" italic="true">method=classical[rk4]. </Font></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.4.1 Exercise</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Investigate the accuracy of these two schemes for low and high values of  <Font style="Text" italic="true">p</Font>. Identify values of the stepsize <Font style="Text" italic="true">h </Font>which give roughly comparable errors for the energy after say <Font style="Text" italic="true">t=10.</Font> What <Font style="Text" italic="true">h</Font> is needed in the forward Euler method to give a comparable level of accuracy? Compare the time taken (use the <Font style="Text" italic="true">time</Font> command introduced on worksheet 3) with these different time steps.</Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.5 Improved accuracy and efficiency with adaptive Runge-Kutta</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Maple's default numerical integration scheme, which it uses if the <Font style="Text" italic="true">method=classical[scheme]</Font> option is omitted, is a Fehlberg fourth-fifth order Runge-Kutta method (also used if you specify <Font style="Text" italic="true">method=rkf45; </Font>see Help, Topic Search, <Font style="Text" italic="true">dsolve,numeric</Font> for more details). The method is implemented using an adaptive grid, so that a small stepsize <Font style="Text" italic="true">h</Font> is used when the dependent variable(s) in the equation change rapidly, and a  larger one when they are vary slowly. In this way the method combines both accuracy and efficiency.</Text-field><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
<Section collapsed="false" isCollapsible="true" drawButton="true" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title><Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">2.5.1 Exercise</Text-field></Title><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal">Solve the mass-on-a-spring system for a large value of <Font style="Text" italic="true">p</Font> and investigate the accuracy of the solution. Compare the time taken by the <Font style="Text" italic="true">rkf45</Font> scheme with that taken by the <Font style="Text" italic="true">rk2</Font> or <Font style="Text" italic="true">rk2 </Font>schemes, for <Font bold="true" foreground="[255,0,0]">comparable levels of accuracy</Font>. Use the Help pages to find out how to control the criterion used by rkf45 when it decides whether or not to change stepsize.</Text-field>
</Section><Text-field style="Normal" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Section>
